<h2 id="reginald-i-cannot">“Reginald, I Cannot”</h2>

<p>In the Dominican house at Naples, in the last weeks of 1273, Brother Thomas of Aquino kept the same order every day. At dawn he said Mass in the chapel of St. Nicholas, and then he heard a second Mass. After that he lectured, and after the lecture he wrote and dictated until dinner. After dinner he went to his cell, and there he wrote again. He was deep in the third part of his <em>Summa</em> of theology, and he had come to the sacrament of penance.</p>

<p>One morning, about the feast of St. Nicholas, he was saying Mass in that chapel when something happened to him. A royal official who later swore to the story said that Thomas “was moved by a wonderful change.” After that Mass he never wrote or dictated another word. In the old Latin phrase, he hung up the instruments of writing.</p>

<p>His companion was Reginald of Piperno, a fellow friar who served him as secretary and confessor. Reginald waited, and then he grew afraid. He wondered whether so much study had turned his master’s mind. He urged him to go back to the work, and Thomas answered, “Reginald, I cannot.” Reginald pressed him again. “Reginald, I cannot, because everything I have written seems to me like straw.”</p>

<p>Then Thomas made Reginald swear an oath. He swore by the living and almighty God, by the faith he kept to their Order, and by the love that bound him to Thomas. As long as Thomas lived, he was to tell no one what he was about to hear. Then Thomas said it plainly.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“Everything I have written seems to me like straw compared with what I have seen and what has been revealed to me.”</p>
  <p><cite>— Thomas Aquinas to Reginald of Piperno, Naples, December 1273, as Bartholomew of Capua swore in 1319 (our translation)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>Reginald kept his promise. Years later he told the story to Bartholomew of Capua, a royal official of Naples who had watched Thomas in those last years. In 1319 Bartholomew repeated it under oath, before the judges who were examining whether Thomas should be declared a saint.</p>

<p>The man who called his work straw had spent his whole adult life on it. He had taught theology at Paris, at Orvieto, at Rome, and at Naples, where the king paid him a monthly ounce of gold to lecture. He had written the <em>Summa contra Gentiles</em>, commentaries on Aristotle and on the Gospels, and, as one of his students recorded, the office for the new feast of Corpus Christi. Kings asked his advice. Within three months of that December morning he was dead, in a Cistercian abbey on the road to a council he never reached. Forty-nine years later the pope declared him a saint. In 1567 the Church named him a Doctor of the Church, and in 1880 a pope made him the patron of every Catholic university and school.</p>

<p>Thomas is the Church’s great teacher of theology, the friar who took the whole of human reason, Aristotle included, and set it to work in the service of faith. He believed, as he wrote, that grace does not destroy nature but perfects it. He argued every question by first setting out the strongest case against his own answer, and he met his opponents with courtesy. Yet the center of his life lay somewhere his books could not reach. The boy who, an old legend says, kept asking his teachers “What is God?” grew into the man who wrote that about God we cannot know what he is, only what he is not. What he saw in the chapel of St. Nicholas, and what he did with it, is the answer to his life. It begins in a castle called Roccasecca, with a storm.</p>

<h2 id="a-boy-meant-for-monte-cassino">A Boy Meant for Monte Cassino</h2>

<p>Thomas was born about 1225 at Roccasecca, or close by, into the large and noble family of Landulf, Count of Aquino, and his wife Theodora. There were brothers named Rainald, Landulf, and Philip, and there were sisters: one would become a countess at San Severino, another an abbess at Capua. Much of what we know of his first years came from Theodora herself. William of Tocco, the Dominican who gathered the evidence for Thomas’s canonization and wrote his life, said that he had it “from the faithful account of his mother.”</p>

<p>She had stories to tell. Before he was born, she said, a hermit of the mountain known as Brother Bonus greeted her: “Rejoice, lady, for you are with child and will bear a son whom you will call Thomas.” The parents would mean him to be a monk and an abbot, the hermit told her, but he would become a Friar Preacher without equal. Long afterward Thomas’s great-niece, Lady Catherine of Morra, swore before Tocco, a judge, and a notary that she had heard this from Theodora’s own lips.</p>

<p>The other story was darker. Lightning struck the tower at Roccasecca. Thomas’s little sister was asleep there, and she was killed, along with the horses in the stable. Theodora ran trembling to the bed where the nurse slept with the baby, and found him unharmed. A third tale, of the kind that gathers around saints’ cradles, tells of an infant who clutched a paper with the Hail Mary on it. He cried, the story goes, until he had a scrap of writing to hold. Tocco set that story at a public bath in Naples; a later biographer moved it home. They are legends, kept by a mother and repeated by friars, and they show at least what the family remembered: a child who held on.</p>

<p>When he was about five, Thomas was taken up the mountain to the abbey of Monte Cassino, with a master to teach him. His father, said one witness in 1319, had made him a monk there, and Landulf meant him to be abbot one day. Tocco describes a quiet child, “not chattering in words,” who drew away from the other noble boys with his lesson sheet in his hand. A legend, marked as legend even by the old writer who passed it on, has him asking his teachers, again and again, “What is God?”</p>

<p>From the mountain he went down to Naples to study. A master named Martin taught him grammar and logic, and Peter of Ireland taught him natural science. Tocco records that a Dominican there saw, three times, a ray of light shining from the boy’s face. In Naples he also met the Friars Preachers, the friars of St. Dominic. A friar named John of San Giuliano encouraged him, and by one account the prior, Thomas Agni of Lentini, gave him the habit. Thomas was still in his teens. The early writers disagree about his exact age, fourteen in one and sixteen in another, and none of them doubts what happened next. Theodora set out for Naples.</p>

<h2 id="not-as-brothers-but-as-enemies">“Not as Brothers but as Enemies”</h2>

<p>The friars moved quickly. They sent their new brother north out of Naples, by Terracina and Anagni, toward their house of Santa Sabina in Rome, meaning to hide him there and then send him on to study in Paris. Theodora sent a messenger to her other sons, who were serving with the emperor Frederick II at Acquapendente, and charged them, by their mother’s blessing, to bring their brother back.</p>

<p>The brothers asked the emperor’s leave, and got it. They set guards on the roads. Somewhere on the way north they found him: Thomas was resting with four friars beside a spring, where the ground was green with water. Tocco, who wrote the scene down, chose his words with care. The brothers fell on the little party “not as brothers, but as enemies.” They tried to tear the habit off him, and they could not, because he held it fast. Ptolemy of Lucca, who later studied under Thomas and travelled with him, tells it a little differently. In his account the brother Rainald, with a man named Peter della Vigna, took Thomas from the company of Master John the Teuton, who was leading him to Rome.</p>

<p>The family had meant him for the abbey, and a friar writing in Thomas’s own lifetime heard that he was offered the abbacy of Monte Cassino itself if he would only put off the habit. To Thomas the habit was his vow. He held on to it at the spring, and he would hold on to it for a long time after. His brothers took him home.</p>

<h2 id="a-burning-brand-and-a-rope">A Burning Brand and a Rope</h2>

<p>They shut him up in the family castles, at Monte San Giovanni and at Roccasecca. Tocco says that his mother kept him there until her sons returned. Bartholomew of Capua, swearing to what he had heard at third hand, named the father instead. Landulf, he said, held his son prisoner for more than a year, and let him go at last because his wife begged him to. Even the length of the captivity was told differently: about a year, one early friar wrote; nearly two years, said Tocco; two or three, said another. Whoever turned the key, it was a long time to be a prisoner in his own home.</p>

<p>The news reached the pope. Innocent IV ordered the emperor to punish the men who had seized a friar on the road, and Frederick arrested the brothers. Then the Dominicans let the matter drop. They did not want a scandal, and one early writer says that Master John the Teuton feared a sentence of blood against the brothers, and spoke to the emperor to soften him. Rainald, the brother whom Ptolemy names, was later killed by Frederick II.</p>

<p>Meanwhile Thomas made a school of his prison. As soon as he was locked in, he asked for a Bible and a breviary. He studied the Bible there, Tocco says, and the <em>Sentences</em>, and it is said that he worked through Aristotle’s book on fallacies too. He taught his sisters. One of them was converted by what he taught her, and she later became an abbess. His brothers tore his habit, and he wrapped himself in the rags of it. Brother John of San Giuliano managed to visit him and secretly gave him a fresh tunic in exchange for the torn one.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“Light came to him in the dark, and in chains he found more freedom.”</p>
  <p><cite>— William of Tocco, on Thomas in captivity, <em>Ystoria sancti Thome de Aquino</em>, c. 1318–1323 (our translation)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>Then came a different test. A lovely girl, dressed as a courtesan, was shut in his room. Thomas seized a burning brand and drove her out. Then he burned the sign of the cross into the wall with it, prayed, and fell asleep. In his sleep, Tocco wrote, two angels came and bound a cord around his waist, saying, “Behold, from God we gird you with the belt of chastity that no temptation can undo.” He cried out in pain. He told no one, Tocco says, until he was close to death, and then only Reginald. A friar writing within Thomas’s lifetime, Thomas of Cantimpré, knew a shorter version without the angels: women were shut in with him, and he stood firmer than before.</p>

<p>In the end the family gave up. Tocco tells it as a mother’s quiet surrender. Theodora pretended not to notice while he was let down from a window by a rope to the friars waiting below. Ptolemy says that Thomas arranged the escape himself, with friars at the castle wall at night. Either way, he came out, still in his habit, and Tocco says he left the castle as if he had spent all that time in the schools. He would soon be sitting in one again, under a teacher named Albert.</p>

<h2 id="the-dumb-ox-of-cologne">The Dumb Ox of Cologne</h2>

<p>The friars sent him to Rome, and from there to study under the Dominican master Albert, known to later ages as Albert the Great. Tocco names Paris and then Cologne; in his sworn testimony of 1319 he mentioned only Cologne. Ptolemy says Thomas was twenty-five when he first came to Paris. Wherever the classroom stood, the student in it was a puzzle to his fellows.</p>

<p>He was very large and very quiet. He studied, he prayed, and he said almost nothing, and the other students decided he was slow. They gave him a nickname, the dumb ox. The story of how the nickname turned against them was first written down some seventy years later, in the lives by Calo, Tocco, and Bernard Gui. None of the witnesses of 1319 tells it, and Tocco himself introduces it with a cautious “it is said.” It is a legend, then, but an old one, and it fits what his fellows saw.</p>

<p>Albert was lecturing on a hard book, the <em>Divine Names</em> of Dionysius. One of the students offered to go over the lecture with Thomas. Partway through, the helper lost his thread. Thomas quietly took it up and finished the explanation himself, and then begged the student to keep the secret. The student told Albert. A page of Thomas’s notes was found as well. The next day Albert set Thomas a difficult question, and then pressed him with four arguments, one after another, and Thomas answered. Then Albert turned to the room.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“We call him the dumb ox, but he will yet give such a bellow in his teaching that it will sound through the whole world.”</p>
  <p><cite>— Albert the Great, as William of Tocco reports with the words “it is said,” c. 1318–1323 (our translation)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>Whatever was said in that room, Albert believed in his student. When the time came for Thomas to take the next step, Albert wrote to the Master of the Order to have him made a bachelor at Paris. The Master hesitated, Tocco says, until Cardinal Hugh urged him on. About 1252, the silent student went to Paris to teach.</p>

<h2 id="water-on-the-mountains">Water on the Mountains</h2>

<p>In about 1256 the Chancellor at Paris ordered that Thomas be made a master of theology. Thomas did not want it. He pleaded that he was too young. The Chancellor would not hear of it. So Thomas prayed the psalm that begins “Save me,” and then he fell asleep.</p>

<p>In his sleep, Tocco writes, an old friar of his Order brought him a verse of the psalms: “Watering the mountains from his heights: the earth shall be filled with the fruit of your works.” A friar who wrote before 1260 had heard it as a dream in which someone handed Thomas a book with the verse in it. Thomas himself told it, at the very end of his life, to the prior of the abbey where he would die. It was a gentle answer to his fear. The water did not come from the mountains. It fell on them from above, and the fruit belonged to God.</p>

<p>He taught at Paris for the next few years. In one of his first works, written as a young man, he had warned that a small error at the start becomes a great one at the end, and his teaching showed that care. In argument, a witness remembered, he never used “boastful or swollen words.” At Paris a master named John Peckham once baited him with sharp words, and Thomas answered him “with sweetness and courtesy.” The same witness heard about a day when Thomas was walking near Paris with some friars, and one of them looked at the city and said, “Father, how beautiful this city of Paris is!” Then he went further. “You could sell it to the king of France, and with the money build all the houses of the Friars Preachers.” Thomas had another treasure in mind. “In truth,” he said, “I would rather have Chrysostom on Matthew.”</p>

<p>He meant a book, the homilies of St. John Chrysostom on the Gospel of Matthew. A councillor of the king of France told the story, and he said it was common knowledge in Paris. It was a fair statement of his ambitions.</p>

<h2 id="the-friar-who-refused-naples">The Friar Who Refused Naples</h2>

<p>In 1259 Thomas went home to Italy, and for most of the next nine years his Order kept him moving. A chapter of the Dominicans at Naples in 1260 named him a preacher general, describing him simply as the friar “who had come back from Paris a master in theology.” In 1261 the chapter at Orvieto assigned him to teach the friars there. In 1265 the chapter at Anagni told him to keep a house of studies in Rome. All through these years he wrote. Among the books of this time was the one now called the <em>Summa contra Gentiles</em>. It opens with a verse of Proverbs, “My mouth shall meditate truth,” and a page later it makes a confession of where his heart lay.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“Of all human pursuits, the pursuit of wisdom is the more perfect, the more sublime, the more useful, and the more agreeable.”</p>
  <p><cite>— Thomas Aquinas, <em>Summa contra Gentiles</em> I.2, tr. Joseph Rickaby, 1905</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>Agreeable, because, as he wrote, quoting the Book of Wisdom, her company has “no bitterness,” and living with her no weariness, only gladness and joy. He also wrote, in the same opening pages, that it belongs to the wise man to set things in order, and that truth must be the last end of the whole universe. In these years too he worked on the <em>Catena aurea</em>, the “golden chain.”</p>

<p>For a time he lived near the papal court. In 1264 Pope Urban IV issued his bull on the feast of Corpus Christi, the feast of the Eucharist, and the feast needed prayers and hymns. Ptolemy of Lucca, who had studied under Thomas, wrote that Thomas composed the office for it at the pope’s command, and modern scholars judge his authorship probable. The <em>Pange lingua</em> sings of the Word made flesh, who by a word makes bread his flesh, and admits that the senses fail before it: to make a sincere heart firm, “faith alone suffices.” Later in the same hymn, in the verses that begin <em>Tantum ergo</em>, the singers bow low to venerate so great a sacrament and ask that faith supply what the senses lack. The matins hymn, <em>Sacris solemniis</em>, calls for a complete renewal: “Let old things pass away, let all be new: hearts, voices, and works.” The sequence <em>Lauda Sion</em> turns that humility into a dare.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“Dare as much as you can, for he is greater than all praise, and you are not able to praise enough.”</p>
  <p><cite>— <em>Lauda Sion</em>, sequence for Corpus Christi, ascribed to Thomas Aquinas (our translation)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>Pope Clement IV wanted to give him more than a liturgy to write. He offered Thomas the archbishopric of Naples and the abbey of San Pietro ad Aram. Thomas refused, and begged the pope not to promote him. He was a friar, and he meant to stay one.</p>

<p>Two small stories come from these years. Ptolemy of Lucca, travelling with Thomas from Rome, stopped with him at Molara, a castle of Cardinal Richard. Thomas had a tertian fever, and his companion lay sick with a fever that never let up. Thomas wore relics of St. Agnes that he had brought from Rome, and he sent them to his companion; the sick man recovered. In Rome, during Holy Week, Thomas preached at St. Peter’s. A woman who had suffered from a flow of blood touched his cloak, and she afterward told his companion that she had been healed. In 1268 his Order sent him back to Paris.</p>

<h2 id="at-the-table-of-the-king">At the Table of the King</h2>

<p>The king of France in those years was Louis IX, the king the Church would also call a saint, and Louis respected the Dominican master enough to ask his advice in hard cases. One day Thomas was invited to dine at the royal table, and as the meal went on he was somewhere else entirely. Suddenly he struck the table. “Now it is settled against the Manichaean heresy!”</p>

<p>The prior who had come with him tugged at his cape. “Take care, Master,” he said. “You are now at the table of the king of France.” Thomas came back to the room. “I had thought long on a question,” he explained, “and now I have found a fine answer.” The king called for a scribe to take the argument down.</p>

<p>The story makes people smile, and it shows how Thomas lived: inside a question. Yet his friends said that the source of the work was prayer. Before every study Thomas prayed, and Reginald said that he prayed with streaming tears. Once, when he had been fasting and praying over a passage of Isaiah, Reginald heard him in the night, talking in the dark. Then Thomas called him. “Son Reginald, get up, light the candle, and take the notebook in which you were writing on Isaiah.” He dictated as if he were reading from a book. Afterward he forbade Reginald, “on God’s behalf,” to tell anyone while he lived. When Reginald pleaded with him, Thomas told him at last that the apostles Peter and Paul had come to him. Reginald kept that secret too.</p>

<p>The book he was writing in these years, and would still be writing in 1273, was the <em>Summa Theologiae</em>. It moves from God, to the moral life and the virtues, to Christ and the sacraments, in hundreds of questions. Each one opens with the strongest arguments against Thomas’s own answer, then gives the answer, then replies to each objection in turn. He opened it by asking whether we need any teaching beyond philosophy at all. It was necessary for our salvation, he answered, that there be a teaching revealed by God besides the philosophy that human reason works out. Yet he would not despise reason. Since grace does not destroy nature but perfects it, he wrote, natural reason must serve faith. Theology uses the other sciences as handmaids, he explained, because of the weakness of our minds, and through no lack of its own. Even a small and uncertain knowledge of God was worth more to him than any certainty about lesser things.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“The least that can be known of the highest things is more to be desired than the most certain knowledge of the smallest things.”</p>
  <p><cite>— Thomas Aquinas, <em>Summa Theologiae</em> I, q. 1, a. 5, ad 1 (our translation)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>In the same reply he admitted the limits of his own mind. When we doubt the things of faith, he wrote, the reason lies in the weakness of the human intellect, which is like an owl’s eye dazzled by the light of the sun. A few pages later he wrote that about God we cannot know what he is, only what he is not. The boy who asked “What is God?” had become the man who wrote down how little could be said.</p>

<p>That did not stop him from reasoning toward God. Early in the <em>Summa</em> he set out five ways by which the mind can climb from the world to its Maker. The first starts from something anyone can see: “It is certain, and evident to the senses, that some things in this world are moved.” The fifth looks at the order of nature, where things are directed to their end as an arrow is directed by the archer. “Therefore there is some intelligent being by whom all natural things are directed to their end,” it concludes, “and this we call God.” It is natural for human beings, he wrote, to come to the things of the mind through the things of the senses, because all our knowledge begins in the senses. So the divine light, as he quoted from Dionysius, can shine on us only veiled in many sacred veils.</p>

<p>What he could say, he said with warmth. To love, he wrote, is to will good to someone, and God loves everything that exists; his love pours out and creates the goodness in things. Even beauty had its place in his scheme: we call beautiful, he wrote, the things that please when seen. A person is called merciful, he observed, as one who has a “miserable heart,” and mercy belongs to God most of all, in what it does, though not as a passion. Among all the virtues that concern our neighbor, he wrote, mercy is the greatest. Charity is a kind of friendship between man and God. He could not resist a plain example of what friendship was not: it would be ridiculous, he said, to speak of friendship with wine or with a horse. His teaching on law was just as plain. Law is an ordinance of reason for the common good, made by the one who has care of the community, and made known. The first precept of the natural law, which he called in one sermon the light of understanding placed in us by God, is that good is to be done and pursued, and evil avoided. Justice is the lasting will to give each person what is his due. Jews and pagans, he wrote, must in no way be forced to believe, because to believe is an act of the will. In the same article, though, he allowed that heretics and apostates might be compelled.</p>

<p>He knew that minds grow tired, and he made room in his great book for rest and play. He retold an old story of St. John the Evangelist and a bow that would break if it were always bent: so too, he wrote, the human mind would break if it were never relaxed. Play, taken like sleep once serious duties are done, even had its own virtue, which he called <em>eutrapelia</em>. In 1272 his Order called him home to Naples, where he had first put on the habit.</p>

<h2 id="a-lent-of-sermons-in-naples">A Lent of Sermons in Naples</h2>

<p>In 1272 the Dominicans gave Thomas charge of their house of studies at Naples. The king, Charles of Anjou, ordered that “Brother Thomas of Aquino,” lecturing in theology there, be paid an ounce of gold a month. His family found him again, this time with paperwork. A royal letter named him executor of the will of his nephew Roger of Aquila, Count of Fondi. In the spring of 1273 his niece Francesca of Ceccano received a royal pass to travel to the baths at Pozzuoli. Among his students in Naples was Ptolemy of Lucca. These were also the years that Bartholomew of Capua saw for himself, and his memory of them is the clearest portrait we have.</p>

<p>Bartholomew could not remember ever seeing Thomas outside the cloister, except once, toward evening. He kept to the order of the day that opens this story, Mass at dawn and then the lecture and the desk. At table a bowl could be set before him and taken away again several times without his noticing. When the friars went to the garden for recreation, he would leave them and go off alone. Tocco says he kept away from the company of women and scolded friars who sat talking with them too long. Yet his face, Bartholomew said, was “cheerful, mild, and gentle.” Another witness, James of Caiazzo, first saw him when he looked about forty-six: a very tall man, bald at the front. He ate one meal a day in the refectory and cared nothing for fine food or fine clothes. Tocco adds that he felt pain more sharply than most men. He tells of a night when Thomas warned a companion, “Whatever you see in me, take care not to call me,” and the candle in his hand burned down to his fingers.</p>

<p>He also preached, and Naples came to hear him. In the Lent of 1273 he preached every day on the Our Father, and a witness said that almost the whole people of the city came. He preached in his own Neapolitan speech, plainly. John Blasi, who also gave evidence in 1319, heard him preach a whole Lent on the Hail Mary, and remembered that Thomas preached with his eyes closed. Tocco adds that he seemed to be looking into heaven. Some of his talks to the people survive because listeners took them down. In the notes Reginald made of his sermons on the Creed, Thomas begins, “The first thing necessary for a Christian is faith.” Believe the things of faith, he tells his hearers, more than what you see, because human sight can be deceived, but God’s knowledge never fails. Another friar, Peter of Andria, took down his sermons on the commandments, which open with three things a person needs for salvation: to know what to believe, what to desire, and what to do.</p>

<p>The same John Blasi swore to a stranger scene. He saw Thomas, in the friary at Naples, raise his fist and rush at a demon, crying, “Why have you come here to tempt me?” The scholar who never used a boastful word in debate had not lost the fighting spirit of the boy who held on to his habit at the spring.</p>

<p>For Thomas, the lecture hall and the pulpit were two ends of one life. In the <em>Summa</em> he put the whole of it in one sentence. Christ himself, he wrote elsewhere in the same book, chose a life in which he handed on to others, by preaching and teaching, what he had contemplated. In his commentary on John he wrote that the other three Gospels teach us about the active life, but John teaches us about the contemplative life as well. John’s contemplation, he said, was high, wide, and complete.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“As it is greater to illuminate than only to shine, so it is greater to hand on to others what one has contemplated than only to contemplate.”</p>
  <p><cite>— Thomas Aquinas, <em>Summa Theologiae</em> II-II, q. 188, a. 6 (our translation)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>The most famous story of these Naples years is also one of the latest. Calo, Tocco, and Gui all tell it, but none of the sworn witnesses of 1319 does, and it should be read as legend. The sacristan of the friary, they say, saw Thomas at prayer in the chapel of St. Nicholas, raised two cubits above the floor, and heard a voice come from the crucifix. “Thomas, you have written well of me. What reward will you take from me for your labor?” And Thomas answered, “Lord, nothing but yourself.” Tocco says that Thomas was then writing the third part of the <em>Summa</em>, on Christ, and that after that day he wrote little. Tocco tells a similar story from Salerno, where a Brother James and his companion saw Thomas raised two cubits from the floor in prayer after Matins. Legend or not, the friars who told these stories understood what he wanted. In December, in the same chapel, something happened that Reginald would keep as a secret for years.</p>

<h2 id="like-straw-beside-what-i-have-seen">Like Straw Beside What I Have Seen</h2>

<p>The sworn account is plain. On a morning about the feast of St. Nicholas, 6 December 1273, Thomas was saying Mass in the chapel when he was moved by a wonderful change. After that Mass he never wrote or dictated anything again. He left the <em>Summa</em> in the treatise on penance, at the fourth article of the ninetieth question of its third part. Reginald’s fear, his pleading, the three-word answer, the oath, and the word “straw” all come down to us through Reginald and Bartholomew alone.</p>

<p>Soon afterward Thomas went to visit his sister, the Countess of San Severino. He hardly spoke to her. It was Reginald who explained: “Since about the feast of St. Nicholas he has been in this state, and since then he has written nothing.”</p>

<p>The early biographers tell the story differently, and their version belongs to that visit. Tocco, Calo, and Gui set the great change at San Severino. There, they say, Thomas was rapt for so long, nearly three days in Gui’s telling, that Reginald took him by the cape and shook him. In their version Thomas said, “The end of my writing has come, for such things have been revealed to me that what I have written and taught seems small to me.” They do not use the word straw. Only Bartholomew’s sworn account, which came from Reginald, does.</p>

<p>Either way, the meaning is the same, and Thomas had prepared his readers for it long before. He had written that the least knowledge of the highest things is worth more than the surest knowledge of small ones. He had written that we cannot know what God is. Now something of the highest things had been shown to him, and by that measure all his careful arguments looked like chaff. He did not take back a word. He simply could not go on. Then a summons came from the pope, and he had to.</p>

<h2 id="a-fallen-tree-on-the-road">A Fallen Tree on the Road</h2>

<p>Pope Gregory X had called a council to meet at Lyons, and he wanted Thomas there. In January 1274 Thomas set out from Naples, carrying with him his treatise against the Greeks. Near Teano, going down by the road of Borgo Nuovo, he struck his head on a fallen tree and was left half stunned.</p>

<p>Reginald talked to him about the council, and told him that he and Brother Bonaventure would be made cardinals. Thomas would have none of it. “Reginald,” he said, “in no state can I be as useful to our Order as in this one.” And again: “Reginald, be sure that I will never change my state.” Bartholomew, who told the story, added that Thomas had prayed that he would never change his state and never love earthly things.</p>

<p>They did not get far. At Maenza, the castle of his niece Francesca and her husband, Annibaldo of Ceccano, Thomas fell ill. He lost his appetite, and he asked for fresh herring, a fish he had eaten in France. The doctor, John of Piperno, found a basket of fresh herring among the sardines on a mule-load that had come up from Terracina. What happened next depends on who tells it. Peter of Monte San Giovanni, a monk who was there, swore that Thomas ate them. Tocco says that he refused, as David in the Bible once refused water, and Abbot Nicholas, another sworn witness, said that he would not eat.</p>

<p>The prior of the nearby Cistercian abbey of Fossanova, James, rode over with three of his monks, Peter, John of Pedemonte, and Fidelis, and they stayed four or five days. It was to this prior that Thomas told the story of the old friar and the verse about the mountains. Then he made a decision. “If the Lord means to visit me,” he said, “it is better that I be found in a house of religious than in the houses of lay people.” Riding or carried, he made the six miles or so to Fossanova.</p>

<h2 id="this-is-my-rest-forever">This Is My Rest Forever</h2>

<p>At the entrance of the abbey he spoke to Reginald in the words of a psalm. “Son Reginald, this is my rest for ever and ever; here I will dwell, for I have chosen it.” The monks gave him the abbot’s own room. He lay there for about a month.</p>

<p>The monks carried wood for him on their own shoulders, and it embarrassed him. “Why is this granted to me,” he asked, “that servants of God should serve a man?” They asked him to explain the Song of Songs to them, and he did, briefly.</p>

<p>When Reginald was troubled, Tocco says, Thomas told him a secret. He had asked God, among his other desires, that he might never be raised to any high office, and God had granted it. “Do not be troubled about this, son,” he said. He also made a last general confession of his whole life.</p>

<p>Toward the end, in Lent, he received Communion for the last time. Monks, Dominicans, and Franciscans were there. Tocco says that Thomas was on the floor to receive it, and that he answered the abbot with a profession of faith. “If any knowledge greater than faith can be had of this sacrament in this life,” he said, “I answer in that knowledge that I do believe, and know for certain, that this is true God and true man.” Then, as Tocco gives it, he spoke to the Host.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“I receive you, the price of my soul’s redemption; I receive you, provision for my pilgrimage, for love of whom I have studied, kept watch, and worked; you I have preached and taught. Against you I never said anything; if I said anything, I said it in ignorance, and I am not stubborn in my own opinion.”</p>
  <p><cite>— Thomas Aquinas at Fossanova, March 1274, as William of Tocco gives it (our translation)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>If he had said anything wrongly about this sacrament or the others, he went on, he left it all to the correction of the Holy Roman Church, “in whose obedience I now pass from this life.” Peter of Monte San Giovanni, who stood in the room, remembered them more briefly. Under oath, he gave them so: “I have taught and written much on this most sacred Body and the other sacraments, in the faith of Christ and of the Holy Roman Church, to whose correction I submit and lay open everything.”</p>

<p>He had called his writing straw. Here, over the Host, he said what it had been for. He had studied, kept watch, and worked for love of the One he was receiving, and now he gave the work back to the Church, keeping nothing for himself. Peter remembered that he lived three days after that Communion. He received the last anointing, and soon afterward, in the early morning of 7 March 1274, he died. Tocco says he was in the forty-ninth year of his life.</p>

<h2 id="the-bones-the-masters-asked-for">The Bones the Masters Asked For</h2>

<p>Word went out from Fossanova, and people came. The bishop of Terracina, Francis, a Franciscan, came for the funeral with four or five of his brother Franciscans. Many Dominicans came, and nobles of the Campania, and about a hundred monks and lay brothers of the abbey. Thomas’s niece Francesca could not enter, because women were not allowed inside the monastery. She asked that the bier be carried to the gate, and there she mourned him with two of her women. Tocco, and only Tocco, adds a strange touch: the mule Thomas had ridden broke its rope, walked to the bier, and fell dead “with no other illness.”</p>

<p>A Dominican who had long been Thomas’s confessor preached at the funeral. In his life of Thomas, Tocco says it was Reginald; in his own sworn testimony, he named Peter of Sezze. The preacher said something that closed the secret of the burning brand and the angels’ belt, kept for so many years. He had heard Thomas’s confession of his whole life, he told the mourners, and “I found him as pure as a child of five years, for he never felt the corruption of his flesh.”</p>

<p>The monks had their own testimony. The subprior of Fossanova, John of Ferentino, had been blind for about twenty days. Peter of Monte San Giovanni, who was there, swore that John put his eyes to the dead man’s eyes, and saw again. At Fossanova, Tocco adds, a monk dreamed at the hour of death that a star fell on the monastery and that two others joined it. Far away in Naples, a friar named Paul of Aquila dreamed at that hour that he saw St. Paul come into the schools and lead Thomas out.</p>

<p>In Paris, the news reached the masters of the Faculty of Arts, the teachers of philosophy. On 2 May 1274 they wrote to the general chapter of the Dominicans, then meeting at Lyons. They called Thomas “the morning star, preeminent in the world.” They asked for his unfinished philosophical works. And they asked for something more. “The bones of him now dead,” they wrote, “we humbly ask of you as the greatest gift.”</p>

<p>The monks of Fossanova had no intention of giving them up. They buried Thomas before the high altar, and then, afraid that the Dominicans or the pope would take the body away, they moved it secretly by night to the chapel of St. Stephen. About seven months later, Thomas appeared in a dream to the head of the house and said, “Bring me back to my former place.” The monks opened the grave with iron tools, and a fragrance, “as of many spices,” filled the chapel and the cloister, and monks came running. The body lay whole in its habit, though Thomas had been a big, heavy man and the ground was wet and deep. Only the tip of the nose showed a small crack. Tocco adds that when a mason named Jacobus lifted the earth that had fallen beside the chest, it too smelled sweet, and a nobleman carried some of it away as a relic. They carried Thomas back to the high altar, to a marble tomb. As they did so the cantor began, by chance, the words of the Mass for a confessor: “The mouth of the just shall meditate wisdom.” Thomas had opened his <em>Summa contra Gentiles</em> with almost the same words.</p>

<p>In the fourteenth year after his death the tomb was opened once more, at the request of his sister, the Countess of San Severino. The abbot, Peter of Monte San Giovanni, found the fragrance again, and the body whole except for the tip of the nose. Afraid, he felt compelled to cut off the right hand for her. She received it, Tocco says, devoutly and with tears, and had it carefully kept. Later a Count Thomas of San Severino gave the hand to the Dominicans of Salerno. In the years that followed, people from the towns around Fossanova came to pray at the tomb, and some of them said they had been healed there.</p>

<h2 id="a-galley-off-monte-argentario">A Galley off Monte Argentario</h2>

<p>More than forty years after his death, the Dominicans of the province of Sicily met in chapter at Gaeta. They gave William of Tocco and Robert of Benevento the work of gathering the evidence for his canonization. Tocco himself tells what happened on the way to the papal court at Avignon. A storm caught their galley at sea and drove it toward the rocks of Monte Argentario. The two friars were afraid of losing the miracle records they carried, and they called on Thomas to save them. A new wind rose and turned the ship away.</p>

<p>At Avignon, Pope John XXII received the petition, and Tocco reports what he said.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“We believe that Brother Thomas is a glorious saint in heaven, because his life was holy, and his teaching could not have been without miracle.”</p>
  <p><cite>— Pope John XXII, as William of Tocco reports, c. 1318 (our translation)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<p>The pope was careful as well as warm. In a second consistory he said that he would count it a great glory for the Church to inscribe Thomas among the saints, provided the miracles were found. To his cardinals he said more. Thomas, he told them, had enlightened the Church more than all the other Doctors. A man would profit more from his books in one year than from the teaching of others in a whole lifetime.</p>

<p>In 1318 the pope named the judges: Humbert, the aged archbishop of Naples, Angelo, bishop of Viterbo, and a notary, Pandulf of Sabello. While Tocco waited for the old archbishop, he spent four months at Fossanova gathering accounts of healings at the tomb. In the summer of 1319 the commission heard sworn witnesses at Naples, among them Tocco, John Blasi, and Bartholomew of Capua with the story Reginald had kept. In 1321 a second inquiry sat at Fossanova. On 18 July 1323, at Avignon, after the <em>Veni Creator</em> was sung, the bull of canonization was read. It set out ten miracles, beginning with the fragrant, whole body in the chapel of St. Stephen, and it fixed his feast on 7 March, the day of his death. His life, the bull said, had witnessed to his miracles, and his miracles to his life.</p>

<p>The other nine were healings. A surgeon who had suffered from gout for ten years was cured at the tomb, and Tocco says he afterward rode a horse. A lay brother of Fossanova, whose arm had hung in a sling for three months, fell asleep on the tomb and woke healed. A girl with a swollen throat was touched with relics wrapped in a cloth, and she ate bread. A child of four with a tumor on the back and legs was laid on the tomb and was cured without the surgeon’s knife. Bernard Gui collected more than a hundred such accounts, and he ended his list with a plain admission. God had worked many other miracles through the merits of St. Thomas Aquinas, in different parts of the world, “which are not written in this treatise.”</p>

<p>The body did not rest at Fossanova. In 1348 it passed to the Count of Fondi, and twenty years later Pope Urban V gave it to the Dominicans. When the Master of the Order, Elias of Toulouse, asked for it, he gave the pope a family’s reason: “Holy Father, he is our brother and our flesh.” On 28 January 1369 the relics entered Toulouse, in the presence of Louis of Anjou and, it was said, a hundred and fifty thousand people. After many moves they rest again, since 1974, in the church of the Jacobins.</p>

<p>The Church went on drawing water from his mountains. Within a few years of the canonization the Dominicans set him second only to St. Dominic in their calendar. In 1567 Pope Pius V named him a Doctor of the Church and ordered that his feast be kept with the same rank as those of the four great Latin Doctors. Popes made him principal patron of Naples, and in 1603 a bone of his left arm came to its cathedral. In 1879 Pope Leo XIII, in his encyclical <em>Aeterni Patris</em>, recalled a time when “Thomas reigned supreme” and teachers and taught alike rested “in wonderful harmony” under his authority. The next year he made Thomas patron of every Catholic university, academy, college, and school. In 1969 his feast was moved from 7 March, which so often falls in Lent, to 28 January, the day he came to Toulouse.</p>

<p>The hymns of Corpus Christi that Ptolemy of Lucca credited to him confess, as Thomas did at Fossanova, that the senses fail and faith alone suffices. Among the prayers that tradition gives him, and that modern scholars think probably his, is the <em>Adoro te devote</em>, a long gaze at the hidden Christ in the Eucharist. “Sight, touch, and taste are deceived in you,” it says, “but by hearing alone it is safe to believe.” It remembers his namesake, the apostle who doubted: “I do not see the wounds, as Thomas did, yet I confess you my God.” One verse of it asks the question that began to be answered for him in the chapel of St. Nicholas, in December 1273.</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>“Jesus, whom now I see veiled, when will that come about for which I thirst so much?”</p>
  <p><cite>— <em>Adoro te devote</em>, ascribed to Thomas Aquinas (our translation)</cite></p>
</blockquote>

<h2 id="a-note-on-the-sources">A Note on the Sources</h2>

<p>Thomas’s life is known chiefly from two kinds of record. One is the sworn testimony taken at Naples in 1319. The other is the lives written by Peter Calo, William of Tocco, and Bernard Gui in the years around the canonization, which add many stories that no sworn witness tells. The dumb-ox scene and the speaking crucifix are first found in those lives, and Tocco introduces the ox story with “it is said.” For the word “straw” there is one sworn account, Bartholomew of Capua’s, which came from Reginald. An earlier reading of the process suggested that Bartholomew heard it through a third friar about 1303; that chain was not checked. The sources also disagree on whether his mother or his father held him prisoner, on who arranged his escape, on whether he ate the herrings at Maenza, and on who preached at his funeral. Each version is given here with its teller. The year of his father’s death, which might settle the first question, was not checked. His birth year is given as about 1225, from Tocco’s statement that he died in his forty-ninth year; other writers give 1227. The early lives also differ on his age when he took the habit and on how many days passed between his last Communion and his death.</p>

<p>The Corpus Christi hymns and the <em>Adoro te devote</em> are ascribed to him, and modern editors judge his authorship probable, not certain. Because the exact text of the <em>Panis angelicus</em> stanza was not checked, it is not quoted. Several sayings often credited to him are not his or are unproven. “Beware the man of one book” was first credited to him by Jeremy Taylor in 1649. “To one who has faith, no explanation is necessary” is not found in his works and was made popular by the 1943 film of <em>The Song of Bernadette</em>. No source in his works was found for “The things that we love tell us what we are.” The letter on how to study, often printed as his, is listed by the Corpus Thomisticum as the work of an unknown author. The prayers <em>Creator ineffabilis</em> and <em>Concede mihi</em> are traditionally attributed to him, but no proof either way was found. A later tradition says that he worked as many miracles as he wrote articles; no early source for that line was found.</p>

<h2 id="how-this-was-researched">How This Life Was Researched</h2>

<p>This life was written from twenty-two sources in Latin, English, Italian, and French. Its core is the sworn testimony of the canonization inquiry held at Naples in 1319, read in Latin. Among the witnesses are Bartholomew of Capua, John Blasi, James of Caiazzo, Peter of Monte San Giovanni, Abbot Nicholas, and William of Tocco himself. Beside it stand the early lives by Calo, Tocco, and Gui, and the chronicle of Ptolemy of Lucca, who studied under Thomas. Thomas of Cantimpré’s short account was written while Thomas was alive. Thomas’s own words were read in Latin in the two <em>Summae</em> and seven other works, offices, and sermon reports, and checked against the public-domain English translations of 1905 and 1920. The research gathered thirty-six quotations from his works and twenty-five from witnesses. It marked several details as new to popular English pages, among them the sworn chain behind the hermit’s prophecy, his wish for Chrysostom on Matthew, and the Paris masters’ request for his bones. Several sources were not read: the Fossanova testimonies of 1321, Gui’s chapters on the death, and the full Naples process. Nor were Pius XI’s encyclical <em>Studiorum Ducem</em>, the modern studies of Torrell and Weisheipl, his commentary on the <em>Sentences</em>, his <em>Quodlibets</em>, the Latin text of the bull, or the <em>Acta Sanctorum</em>.</p>

